    "q-2873": {
      "dom_id": "step-q2873",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "$\\sqrt{9{a}^{2}}=$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that $9$ and ${a}^{2}$ are both perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9{a}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}\\cdot {a}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3\\cdot 3\\cdot a\\cdot a} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3\\cdot a\\cdot 3\\cdot a} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3a\\cdot 3a} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3a)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(3a)^{2}} & =|3a| \\\\  & =|3|\\cdot |a| \\\\  & =3|a|\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
